Review of 9bfe70d and 0045992 (pacing uses the capacity it has).
Findings fixed: one 100 ms /proc/stat reading is off by up to a tenth (a CPU 81% busy read 78% to 90% in simulation), so the CPU cap averages the last three readings and cuts at once only on a full-CPU reading. The CPU cap's repeat cut at 50% dropped it to the floor after a first cut that was only slightly short, so CPU cuts are always to 80%, repeated after 300 ms if needed. Probes every 10 s would log the allowance cut warning about 5 times a minute at the limit, so it is logged once a minute and counted in cap_cuts. A failed /proc/stat read no longer retries every 10 ms poll. The committed campaign README said pacing-on resumed at the learned cap; it restarts from the configured cap, as designed and tested.
Checked: go test -race ./... on darwin; golangci-lint for darwin, linux, and linux with privileged,campaign,localfixture (only the pre-existing errcheck hits); privileged tests and the full 22-case campaign in the Lima VM, all pass. The new bench-trace, flood, probe and cliff tests fail against c98c238.
Safety claim: the CPU cap only cuts on the same forwarding-explained checks as before, and a flood is still cut at the end of the first 100 ms reading of a full CPU, proven in the quantized /proc/stat simulation, not yet on AWS. Remaining risk: the 70-80% band and 10%/s raise are tuned in simulation only.
